    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="17" relname="concession">I have to agree with you on Macromedia MX use of setting up the site (local server, remote server, test server, etc.).</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="19" relname="span">However, Dreamweaver is not for the "ill-of-heart."</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="2" relname="evidence">It can overburden someone who has little experience with web development software.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="19" relname="evidence">With the properties, css, assets, files, tag inspector, code inspector, database, server components, and other windows, I have seen many abandon the software and move back to FrontPage (blahh).</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="20" relname="span">In addition, WYSWYG interface is a bit tricky building for multiple browsers and OS.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="5" relname="elaboration">I have found the  very useful before the</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="21" relname="span">Overall, Macromedia MX Dreamweaver is an "excellent" web developer's tool.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="9" relname="condition">If you have not tried the 2004,</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="15" relname="span">give it a shot!!</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="14" relname="summary">I am hoping Macromedia will improve their other software in the near future.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="12" relname="joint">P.S.  I am still searching for what MX stands for.</segment>
        <group id="12" type="multinuc"/>
        <group id="13" type="span" parent="12" relname="joint"/>
